00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:06.349 align:start position:0%
Geof Astle Jimmy Hill Nat Lofthouse and  Billy McNeill some of the most famous  names in football history they all  suffered with neurodegenerative diseases  now a landmark study has shown former  professionals are far more likely to die  from these conditions the family of  former England footballer Jeff Estelle  has long fought to prove his death was  linked to heading of the ball this puts  them a step closer to that but offers a  little comfort the study compared the  health records of X professional  footballers born between 1900 and 1976  with the general population  it found that X footballers were three  and a half times more likely to have  died from neurodegenerative diseases  five times more likely to have died of  Alzheimer's disease and four times as  likely from motor neurone disease but  the report also showed that footballers  live on average over three years longer  and were less likely to die of heart or  lung disease throw the ball a little bit  higher and try to hit it on the same  spot off you go  the FA now issues advice on safe heading  of the ball the families of hundreds of  suffering players are now calling for  more support are you worried that there  could be legal challenges from from the  families no from our point of view the  families we've spoken to them they are  grateful and we've done the research  they're grateful that we're trying to  find an answer to this question that's  been around football for a long time we  actually are making them part of the  process moving forward experts want  further research to determine the risk  to modern-day players it is a  significant step forward I think it's  the first step and now we have that  evidence that shows that there is an  increased risk of dementia or dying of  dementia in X professional football  players now we can take the next steps  and really  understand through further research what  the reasons are underpinning this  increased risk an untold number of  players have died with neurodegenerative  diseases the hope is that this study may  prevent further tragedy Martha Kellner  Sky News
 

